Dear Sir or Madam,

Demo map anchor: M=C3=BChlentalstrasse 185, 8200 Schaffhausen. The address is=
 real; the business, premises and loss are fictional. AI images do not depict=
 the actual building or its occupants. Street View shows the real location, n=
ot these damage scenes.

Further to Martina Gerber's first notice of 28 August and the visit of your l=
oss adjuster, Mr Thomas Wyss, on 31 August (your claim no. PIQ-S-2026-082853,=
 policy PIQ-CH-KMU-2026-071506), please find attached our signed claim form, =
the cost estimate and the ten photos.

Our consolidated loss estimate amounts to CHF 14,968,600.00:
- Movements in assembly, haute complication workshop: 312 calibres (incl. 38 =
tourbillons, 21 minute repeaters and 64 perpetual calendars): CHF 3,960,000.00
- Components and semi-finished parts (main plates, bridges, wheels, hairsprin=
gs, jewels) in the clean room and the assembly store: CHF 1,240,000.00
- Finished watches in final inspection: 186 water-exposed watches in gold and=
 platinum cases (internal damage subject to inspection): CHF 3,540,000.00
- Dials, hands and sapphire crystals in the final assembly store (incl. 64 gr=
and feu enamel dials): CHF 610,000.00
- Parts manufacturing machines: two five-axis machining centres (controls and=
 electrical cabinets), a wire EDM machine and a laser welding station: CHF 2,=
260,000.00
- Watchmaker benches, microscopes, timing machines and tools (workshop, final=
 inspection); mobile laminar-flow benches in the clean room: CHF 690,000.00
- Measuring and test equipment (optical measuring machine, water-resistance t=
ester, rate testing installation) and IT in the workshop: CHF 420,000.00
- Business interruption: loss of gross profit and extra costs until the works=
hop and the clean room restart (planned 30 November 2026): CHF 2,150,000.00
- Emergency measures and clean-up for the contents (water extraction, drying,=
 protective covers, disposal): CHF 98,600.00

Photographic scope and limits:

The photos show selected areas, not a complete inventory: two assembly statio=
ns and two damaged panes in one rooflight section, three movements in close-u=
p, four finished watches lying in a flat work tray, one open machining centre=
 and six bagged sample parts numbered 1001 to 1006. The next-day atelier view=
 shows two dehumidifiers and one air mover after loose glass and instruments =
were removed.

The torn insulation visible on the flat roof belongs to the opaque roof assem=
bly, not the rooflight glazing. The atelier's opaque interior linings remain =
closed; water-damaged ceiling tiles have fallen in the lower-floor rooms. Rai=
n and residual water continued entering and draining after the peak gusts. Em=
ergency roof covering started at 14:30; the 14:32 photo shows work in progres=
s, not completed sealing. The atelier's two damaged panes are covered in the =
next-day view. Drying equipment uses a separate protected supply. Room lighti=
ng is restored for the 31 August visit; the inspected machining centre remain=
s isolated.

Exterior droplets do not establish moisture inside sealed cases. The photos d=
o not establish irreparability, precious-metal fineness or repair costs. The =
reported totals of 312 calibres, 186 finished watches and 24 workstations cov=
er the wider inventory, not just the objects visible in these photos. Other s=
tock, machines and business interruption are not fully photographed. The inve=
ntory lists and technical inspection reports, supplier quotes and business re=
cords must support the claimed quantities, repair scope and valuation; these =
records are available on request, not included in the attached files. The rep=
air allowance, including the proposed watch revisions, remains subject to the=
 loss adjuster's technical and financial review.

For your assessment:

1. The building damage (roof, skylights, ceilings and the clean-room envelope=
) is handled by the cantonal building insurer GVSH under claim no. SH-2026-09=
12 and is not included. The emergency roof cover is invoiced to the GVSH.

2. Movements, components, finished watches and dials are valued at manufactur=
ing cost under AVB A3.1, not at retail prices. Machines and equipment are val=
ued at replacement cost based on the suppliers' quotes and their diagnosis of=
 2 September.

3. The inventory was counted with our statutory auditors on 29 and 30 August;=
 every movement is traced by its serial number in our ERP. The damaged parts =
are kept numbered and packaged for your loss adjuster.

4. Our contents sum insured of CHF 62,000,000.00 exceeds the insurable value =
of CHF 57,860,000.00 at 30 June 2026, the business interruption estimate stay=
s within the CHF 36,000,000.00 sum insured, and the clean-up costs within the=
 CHF 100,000 first-loss limit.

5. We note the Elementar deductible of CHF 50,000.00 (10 %, capped). The resu=
lting estimated net is CHF 14,918,600.00, subject to coverage, damage-scope a=
nd valuation review.

6. To limit the interruption, final assembly moved to our south workshop on 1=
4 September; the haute complication workshop and the clean room are planned t=
o restart on 30 November 2026.

The inventory lists, the suppliers' quotes, the restoration company's invoice=
, our 2026 budget and our 2024 and 2025 accounts are available on request.

